vps-ready <version> on Debian GNU/Linux 13 (trixie) (vm-other)
Audit: 14 passed, 13 failed (1 high, 5 medium, 7 low), 5 unknown. vps-ready can fix 11 of them.

? What will this server run?
  > Something else: only SSH is open (recommended: no Docker or web server found)
    Web apps or Docker: opens ports 80 and 443
? Admin user for logging in: admin
  existing account (the one you logged in as): keeps its sudo rules and logs in
  with 1 key from /home/admin/.ssh/authorized_keys
  Root and password logins will be turned off, so this key is how you log in.
? Private access for SSH
    None: SSH stays reachable from the internet, with keys only
  > Tailscale (the private profile)
  This login comes through tailscale0, so this apply closes SSH to the internet
  and accepts it only through tailscale0.
  If tailscale0 stops working, SSH is closed. Your provider's web console logs
  in with a password, so make sure a sudo user has one (`sudo passwd admin`);
  without it, recovery needs the provider's rescue mode. From the console, `sudo
  systemctl stop vps-ready-firewall` opens everything until the next boot, and
  `sudo vps-ready rollback --txn <id>` (see `sudo vps-ready history`) undoes
  this change. While SSH depends on tailscale0, every later change that arms the
  safety timer needs a confirm through tailscale0, so one applied from the
  console while tailscale0 is down rolls back.
  An auth key from the Tailscale admin console logs this server in during the
  apply. It is used once and never saved.
? Auth key (hidden; Enter to log in yourself after): 
All other incoming ports are blocked.
? More ports to open, e.g. 8080/tcp or 53/udp (Enter for none): 
? Pick which modules to run yourself? (y/N) no

After you apply, you have 5 minutes to log in through tailscale0 from a new
terminal and confirm. If you don't, every change is undone.

user.admin: Set up an admin account that logs in with an SSH key and uses sudo: no changes
  note: no `authorized_keys` given: copies 1 key from /home/admin/.ssh/authorized_keys
  note: admin already has sudo rules (`sudo -l -U admin`); they stay as they are, and no sudoers file is written
ssh.server: Turn off root and password logins over SSH (fixes SSH-001, SSH-002, SSH-005)
  note: SSH sessions that are open now stay open; the new settings apply to new logins
  note: Port, ListenAddress, AllowGroups, AuthenticationMethods and the crypto settings stay as they are
  + /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/00-vps-ready.conf (mode 0644, root:root)       reversible
      @@ -0,0 +1,7 @@
      +# Written by vps-ready (ssh.server). `vps-ready rollback --last`
      +# restores the previous state; edits here are replaced by the next apply.
      +# sshd uses the first value it reads for each keyword, and reads this file
      +# before 50-cloud-init.conf and the rest of sshd_config.
      +PermitRootLogin no
      +PasswordAuthentication no
      +X11Forwarding no
net.tailscale: Join a tailnet with Tailscale, for a private path to SSH
  note: no port is opened: Tailscale needs none, and firewall.inbound's established,related rule covers its replies; tailscaled's own nftables chains (ts-input) coexist with vps-ready's table
  note: this node is logged in already, and `tailscale debug prefs` did not say with which settings; vps-ready leaves it as it is
  + directory /etc/apt/keyrings (mode 0755, root:root)                    reversible
  + /etc/apt/keyrings/tailscale.asc (mode 0644, root:root): PGP public key, fingerprint 2596 A99E AAB3 3821 893C 0A79 458C A832 957F 5868 (52 lines)  reversible
  + /etc/apt/sources.list.d/tailscale.sources (mode 0644, root:root)      reversible
      @@ -0,0 +1,7 @@
      +# Written by vps-ready. `vps-ready rollback --last` removes it.
      +Types: deb
      +URIs: https://pkgs.tailscale.com/stable/debian
      +Suites: trixie
      +Components: main
      +Architectures: amd64
      +Signed-By: /etc/apt/keyrings/tailscale.asc
  + /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/52vps-ready-tailscale (mode 0644, root:root)      reversible
      @@ -0,0 +1,2 @@
      +// Written by vps-ready (net.tailscale). `vps-ready rollback --last` removes it.
      +Unattended-Upgrade::Origins-Pattern { "origin=Tailscale,codename=${distro_codename}"; };
  ~ enable and start tailscaled.service                                   reversible
firewall.inbound: Drop incoming traffic that no rule allows (fixes FW-001)
  note: always accepts TCP 22, the current SSH ports (this session's, root's sshd listeners and `Port`), whatever `allow` says
  note: drops incoming IPv4 TCP that carries IP options, also to containers, to blunt CVE-2026-98432 until the kernel is updated and rebooted; TCP labeled with CIPSO (NetLabel) stops working
  note: tailscaled.service brings tailscale0 up at boot; SSH is unreachable from boot until it is up
  note: if tailscale0 stops working, SSH is closed. Your provider's web console logs in with a password, so make sure a sudo user has one (`sudo passwd admin`); without it, recovery needs the provider's rescue mode. From the console, `sudo systemctl stop vps-ready-firewall` opens everything until the next boot, and `sudo vps-ready rollback --txn <id>` (see `sudo vps-ready history`) undoes this change. While SSH depends on tailscale0, every later change that arms the safety timer needs a confirm through tailscale0, so one applied from the console while tailscale0 is down rolls back
  + directory /etc/vps-ready (mode 0755, root:root)                       reversible
  + /etc/vps-ready/firewall.nft (mode 0644, root:root)                    reversible
      @@ -0,0 +1,33 @@
      +# Written by vps-ready (firewall.inbound). `vps-ready rollback --last`
      +# restores the previous state; edits here are replaced by the next apply.
      +# vps-ready-firewall.service loads it. It replaces only the table
      +# inet vps_ready: other tables, such as Docker's or fail2ban's, stay.
      +table inet vps_ready {}
      +delete table inet vps_ready
      +table inet vps_ready {
      +  # Mitigates CVE-2026-98432, a kernel use-after-free of the IP options of
      +  # a TCP handshake; the fix is a kernel update. Runs before conntrack and
      +  # also sees traffic forwarded to containers. TCP only: IGMP and RSVP use
      +  # the Router Alert option.
      +  chain prerouting {
      +    type filter hook prerouting priority raw; policy accept;
      +    ip protocol tcp ip hdrlength > 5 counter drop
      +  }
      +  chain input {
      +    type filter hook input priority filter; policy drop;
      +    ct state established,related accept
      +    ct state invalid drop
      +    iif lo accept
      +    meta l4proto icmp accept
      +    # Neighbor discovery and path MTU: never block ICMPv6.
      +    meta l4proto icmpv6 accept
      +    # DHCPv4 replies that conntrack does not match (broadcast).
      +    udp sport 67 udp dport 68 accept
      +    # DHCPv6 client.
      +    ip6 saddr fe80::/10 udp dport 546 accept
      +    # SSH only through tailscale0 (`ssh_via`). iifname matches the name at
      +    # packet time, so the rule loads before the interface exists and holds
      +    # after it is recreated.
      +    iifname "tailscale0" tcp dport { 22 } accept
      +  }
      +}
  + /etc/systemd/system/vps-ready-firewall.service (mode 0644, root:root)  reversible
      @@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
      +# Written by vps-ready (firewall.inbound). `vps-ready rollback --last`
      +# restores the previous state; edits here are replaced by the next apply.
      +[Unit]
      +Description=vps-ready: inbound firewall (nftables table inet vps_ready)
      +DefaultDependencies=no
      +Wants=network-pre.target
      +After=nftables.service
      +Before=network-pre.target shutdown.target
      +Conflicts=shutdown.target
      +
      +[Service]
      +Type=oneshot
      +RemainAfterExit=yes
      +ExecStart=/usr/sbin/nft -f /etc/vps-ready/firewall.nft
      +ExecReload=/usr/sbin/nft -f /etc/vps-ready/firewall.nft
      +ExecStop=-/usr/sbin/nft delete table inet vps_ready
      +
      +[Install]
      +WantedBy=sysinit.target
  ~ enable and start vps-ready-firewall.service                           reversible
ssh.bruteforce: Ban addresses that keep failing to log in over SSH
  note: the jail never bans 100.100.0.9, the address of this SSH session
  note: the jail's ignoreip replaces any that jail.local sets
  note: if this installs fail2ban, rollback leaves it installed and enabled, as the package sets it up, so it runs the distro's own jails after a reboot; `systemctl disable --now fail2ban` turns it off
  + /etc/fail2ban/jail.d/90-vps-ready.local (mode 0644, root:root)        reversible
      @@ -0,0 +1,8 @@
      +# Written by vps-ready (ssh.bruteforce). `vps-ready rollback --last`
      +# restores the previous state; edits here are replaced by the next apply.
      +[sshd]
      +enabled = true
      +banaction = nftables
      +backend = systemd
      +port = 22
      +ignoreip = 127.0.0.1/8 ::1 100.100.0.9
  ~ enable and start fail2ban.service                                     reversible
kernel.sysctl: Harden kernel settings (fixes KRN-001, KRN-002, KRN-003, KRN-007, KRN-008, NET-002)
  + /etc/sysctl.d/90-vps-ready.conf (mode 0644, root:root)                reversible
      @@ -0,0 +1,8 @@
      +# Written by vps-ready (kernel.sysctl). `vps-ready rollback --last`
      +# restores the previous state; edits here are replaced by the next apply.
      +kernel.kptr_restrict = 1
      +kernel.dmesg_restrict = 1
      +kernel.unprivileged_bpf_disabled = 2
      +fs.protected_fifos = 1
      +fs.protected_regular = 1
      +net.ipv4.conf.all.accept_source_route = 0
  ~ kernel.kptr_restrict 0 -> 1                                           reversible
  ~ kernel.dmesg_restrict 0 -> 1                                          reversible
  ~ kernel.unprivileged_bpf_disabled 0 -> 2                               reversible
  ~ fs.protected_fifos 0 -> 1                                             reversible
  ~ fs.protected_regular 0 -> 1                                           reversible
  ~ net.ipv4.conf.all.accept_source_route 1 -> 0                          reversible
updates.auto: Install security updates automatically (fixes UPD-001)
  + /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/52vps-ready (mode 0644, root:root)                reversible
      @@ -0,0 +1,3 @@
      +APT::Periodic::Enable "1";
